1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,185
This is the deep dive. The previous guide got you rendering.

2
00:00:07,185 --> 00:00:13,820
This one walks the actual API surface of the alpha libraries, package by package, so you know what exists before you need it.

3
00:00:13,820 --> 00:00:17,802
There are two hundred and seventy three public types across twenty packages

4
00:00:17,802 --> 00:00:19,766
and the useful thing is not the count

5
00:00:19,766 --> 00:00:23,163
it is that maybe fifteen of them matter to a normal integration.

6
00:00:23,163 --> 00:00:26,030
We will name which fifteen, and what the rest are for.

7
00:00:26,030 --> 00:00:32,400
Everything here was read out of the published artifacts, because there is no API reference site for these libraries yet.

8
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:34,205
Here is how the surface splits.

9
00:00:34,205 --> 00:00:40,555
The model artifact is the vocabulary: the protocol types, the processor interfaces, and the function library.

10
00:00:40,555 --> 00:00:43,409
The engine is the state machine that drives them.

11
00:00:43,409 --> 00:00:51,390
Compose runtime is the binding layer, and it carries roughly half its types purely as a property type system, which we will come back to.

12
00:00:51,390 --> 00:00:56,690
Compose UI is small on purpose: twelve types, and it is the contract a renderer implements.

13
00:00:56,690 --> 00:01:00,826
And material 3 dash a 2 u i is the largest of the five, a hundred types

14
00:01:00,826 --> 00:01:04,554
because it is a full catalog implementation: eighteen components

15
00:01:04,554 --> 00:01:08,224
their property declarations, the icon set and theming defaults.

16
00:01:11,480 --> 00:01:13,424
Read this as a map of where to look.

17
00:01:13,424 --> 00:01:17,473
If you are debugging what arrived on the wire, you want model dot protocol.

18
00:01:17,473 --> 00:01:20,713
If the screen is not updating, you want model dot processor.

19
00:01:20,713 --> 00:01:24,007
If a bound value is not refreshing, you want compose runtime.

20
00:01:24,007 --> 00:01:27,463
If a component draws wrong, you want the catalog implementation.

21
00:01:27,463 --> 00:01:31,728
Most alpha debugging is figuring out which of those four layers is lying to you

22
00:01:31,728 --> 00:01:35,184
and knowing the split turns a vague problem into a specific one.

23
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:42,837
Start with the object you will hold a reference to.

24
00:01:42,837 --> 00:01:47,347
The processor interface is four members and that is the entire runtime contract.

25
00:01:47,347 --> 00:01:52,364
Active surfaces is a state flow of surface models, and it is what you collect in Compose.

26
00:01:52,364 --> 00:01:58,113
Outbound events is a flow of client to server messages, which is every user action leaving the client.

27
00:01:58,113 --> 00:02:00,819
Process message takes one parsed server message.

28
00:02:00,819 --> 00:02:03,581
Process error reports a client-side failure back.

29
00:02:03,581 --> 00:02:07,866
And collect messages is a suspending function that drains the inbound queue.

30
00:02:07,866 --> 00:02:12,319
That last one catches people: it is not optional and it is not fire and forget.

31
00:02:12,319 --> 00:02:19,929
If you do not keep it running inside a launched effect for the lifetime of the screen, messages queue up and the surface never changes.

32
00:02:19,929 --> 00:02:23,368
A blank screen with no error in logcat is almost always this.

33
00:02:23,368 --> 00:02:27,878
Note also the factory takes a list of catalogs and a list of action interceptors

34
00:02:27,878 --> 00:02:32,162
and an interceptor gets to rewrite or swallow a user action before it leaves

35
00:02:32,162 --> 00:02:35,995
which is where you would put logging, redaction or an approval step.

36
00:02:39,637 --> 00:02:43,626
Next, the types that define what can travel between an agent and a client.

37
00:02:43,626 --> 00:02:45,959
Eighteen types, and they divide cleanly.

38
00:02:45,959 --> 00:02:52,492
Server to client is four messages: create a surface, update components, update the data model, delete a surface.

39
00:02:52,492 --> 00:02:55,291
Client to server is two: an event, and an error.

40
00:02:55,291 --> 00:03:00,249
That asymmetry is the shape of the protocol. The agent describes, the client reports.

41
00:03:00,249 --> 00:03:05,382
Then the action types. A user action carries the surface, the component and a timestamp.

42
00:03:05,382 --> 00:03:08,532
An event action is the one that travels to your agent.

43
00:03:08,532 --> 00:03:13,490
A function call action does not travel at all, it invokes a catalog function locally.

44
00:03:13,490 --> 00:03:18,273
Underneath sit the supporting types: the client data model with its to payload map

45
00:03:18,273 --> 00:03:21,656
which is how you serialise state to send back; a data path

46
00:03:21,656 --> 00:03:25,272
which is a JSON pointer into the data model; an inline catalog

47
00:03:25,272 --> 00:03:30,988
for sending a catalog definition with the surface instead of by reference; and client capabilities

48
00:03:30,988 --> 00:03:34,021
which is how a client advertises what it can handle.

49
00:03:40,506 --> 00:03:44,630
And here are three of those message types as an agent actually emits them.

50
00:03:44,630 --> 00:03:47,362
Create surface names the surface and the catalog.

51
00:03:47,362 --> 00:03:49,758
Update components carries an adjacency list

52
00:03:49,758 --> 00:03:53,716
where each entry has an identifier and names its children by identifier

53
00:03:53,716 --> 00:03:55,555
with the entry point called root.

54
00:03:55,555 --> 00:03:57,617
Update data model carries the values.

55
00:03:57,617 --> 00:04:02,188
Notice this agent sends the components in several update messages rather than one.

56
00:04:02,188 --> 00:04:03,358
Nothing requires that

57
00:04:03,358 --> 00:04:07,316
but because components refer to children by name rather than by nesting

58
00:04:07,316 --> 00:04:11,106
a partial tree is perfectly legal, and the client draws what it has.

59
00:04:17,266 --> 00:04:20,270
Now the part of the API that is largest and least discussed.

60
00:04:20,270 --> 00:04:27,789
Roughly twenty eight of the compose runtime types are property declarations, and they are how a component describes its own schema.

61
00:04:27,789 --> 00:04:33,758
Every one extends A2UI property, which carries a key, a JSON schema fragment and whether it is required.

62
00:04:33,758 --> 00:04:36,513
The split that matters is static versus dynamic.

63
00:04:36,513 --> 00:04:42,081
A static property is fixed when the component is sent: a string, a number, an enum, a child list.

64
00:04:42,081 --> 00:04:46,328
A dynamic property may instead carry a path, binding it to the data model.

65
00:04:46,328 --> 00:04:51,207
Only dynamic properties can be bound, and only bound properties take part in updates.

66
00:04:51,207 --> 00:04:53,331
So when you design a custom component

67
00:04:53,331 --> 00:04:56,488
the question for each property is not what type is this

68
00:04:56,488 --> 00:04:59,817
it is can an agent bind this to data, or is it structural.

69
00:04:59,817 --> 00:05:03,433
Get that wrong and the component renders once and never reacts.

70
00:05:03,433 --> 00:05:05,212
Notice also the enum properties

71
00:05:05,212 --> 00:05:11,641
string enum and number enum: declaring a property that way is what produces the strict validation we hit earlier

72
00:05:11,641 --> 00:05:15,716
where an unexpected variant renders an error chip rather than throwing.

73
00:05:17,110 --> 00:05:19,695
Which brings us to the piece you probably came here for.

74
00:05:19,695 --> 00:05:21,860
Thirteen functions ship in the alpha

75
00:05:21,860 --> 00:05:26,370
and they all implement one interface: a definition describing the signature

76
00:05:26,370 --> 00:05:30,399
and an execute that takes an argument map and an execution context.

77
00:05:30,399 --> 00:05:35,390
They group into four kinds. Validation: required, email, numeric, regex and length.

78
00:05:35,390 --> 00:05:40,381
Logic: and, or and not, which let an agent compose conditions without sending code.

79
00:05:40,381 --> 00:05:45,492
Formatting: format string, format number, format currency, format date and pluralize.

80
00:05:45,492 --> 00:05:49,220
And one side effect: open URL. Two details matter in practice.

81
00:05:49,220 --> 00:05:56,917
First, the stateless ones are Kotlin objects, so you reference required function dot instance rather than constructing anything.

82
00:05:56,917 --> 00:06:02,329
The ones that need locale or a message formatter take that dependency in their constructor

83
00:06:02,329 --> 00:06:07,139
and that is precisely why the Material catalog factory demands a locale provider

84
00:06:07,139 --> 00:06:09,905
a message formatter and a URL opener from you.

85
00:06:09,905 --> 00:06:12,731
It is constructing this library on your behalf.

86
00:06:12,731 --> 00:06:16,760
Second, and this is the important one: these execute on the client.

87
00:06:16,760 --> 00:06:22,232
When an agent marks a field required, or asks for a currency format, no round trip happens.

88
00:06:22,232 --> 00:06:24,397
Validation is immediate and offline.

89
00:06:24,397 --> 00:06:29,448
You can look any of them up by name from a catalog's function collection at runtime.

90
00:06:29,448 --> 00:06:31,662
So how does a component get registered at all.

91
00:06:31,662 --> 00:06:35,383
This is the whole renderer contract, and it is two interfaces.

92
00:06:35,383 --> 00:06:36,703
A component has a name

93
00:06:36,703 --> 00:06:42,285
which is the string an agent puts in the component field; a description; a list of properties

94
00:06:42,285 --> 00:06:45,646
which is the schema we just walked; an optional is ready

95
00:06:45,646 --> 00:06:49,547
which lets a component decline to draw until its data has arrived

96
00:06:49,547 --> 00:06:54,108
and that is how streaming avoids flicker; and a composable content function.

97
00:06:54,108 --> 00:06:58,429
A catalog has an identifier, which is the catalog identifier on the wire

98
00:06:58,429 --> 00:07:02,870
a collection of components, a collection of functions, and a theme schema.

99
00:07:02,870 --> 00:07:06,411
Both collections are lists with lookup by name. That is it.

100
00:07:06,411 --> 00:07:11,453
Nothing in either interface mentions Material, Compose theming or any design system.

101
00:07:11,453 --> 00:07:15,114
Material 3 dash a 2 u i is one implementation of A2UI catalog

102
00:07:15,114 --> 00:07:20,395
and because the processor takes a list, yours can sit next to it rather than replace it.

103
00:07:35,862 --> 00:07:38,539
Inside a component, six functions do the work.

104
00:07:38,539 --> 00:07:43,208
The component scope is what your content function receives, and it is six members.

105
00:07:43,208 --> 00:07:47,534
Bind reads a dynamic property and recomposes when the underlying data moves.

106
00:07:47,534 --> 00:07:49,299
Bind updater hands you a setter

107
00:07:49,299 --> 00:07:56,644
and that is two-way binding: a text field calls it on every keystroke and the client data model updates with no network involved.

108
00:07:56,644 --> 00:08:00,913
Bind child references resolves a child list into references you can render.

109
00:08:00,913 --> 00:08:05,468
Observe component state gives you a component's state by surface and identifier.

110
00:08:05,468 --> 00:08:11,275
Dispatch action fires a user action, which is what surfaces on outbound events and reaches your agent.

111
00:08:11,275 --> 00:08:13,552
Report error pushes a client error back.

112
00:08:13,552 --> 00:08:19,929
Alongside it, component properties gives typed access to static values, with a contains check for optional ones.

113
00:08:19,929 --> 00:08:27,501
In practice a custom component is mostly three of these: bind for reading, bind updater for writing, dispatch action for interaction.

114
00:08:31,499 --> 00:08:34,159
Here is the shape a component implementation takes.

115
00:08:34,159 --> 00:08:40,730
These are the media stubs the Material catalog requires, so they are deliberately empty, but the structure is what to look at.

116
00:08:40,730 --> 00:08:45,320
Typed content is an extension function on the component scope rather than a plain method

117
00:08:45,320 --> 00:08:49,388
which means the binding functions are in scope inside it without being passed.

118
00:08:49,388 --> 00:08:51,474
The accessibility parameter is nullable.

119
00:08:51,474 --> 00:08:58,254
And the property list is typed, so the values arriving here have already been validated against the schema the component declared.

120
00:09:29,779 --> 00:09:31,707
And the implementation you get for free.

121
00:09:31,707 --> 00:09:36,169
The Material artifact exposes a small public surface over a large private one.

122
00:09:36,169 --> 00:09:39,544
A2UI surface is the composable you hand a surface model to.

123
00:09:39,544 --> 00:09:43,435
Surface defaults and Material A2UI defaults carry the theming knobs.

124
00:09:43,435 --> 00:09:47,382
And the catalog factory takes those six parameters we have discussed.

125
00:09:47,382 --> 00:09:54,591
Behind that sit roughly a hundred types: eighteen components, their property declarations, an icon set and the theme plumbing.

126
00:09:54,591 --> 00:09:59,683
Worth saying plainly: if your design system is not Material, you are not stuck with this.

127
00:09:59,683 --> 00:10:01,971
You implement the same catalog interface

128
00:10:01,971 --> 00:10:09,294
and the hundred types behind this one are the scale of work you are taking on if you implement all eighteen components yourself.

129
00:10:30,307 --> 00:10:34,058
With the API named, the behaviour we saw earlier reads differently.

130
00:10:34,058 --> 00:10:40,328
This form arriving in pieces is create surface, then several update components messages, then update data model.

131
00:10:40,328 --> 00:10:46,150
The fields fill last because binding resolves against a data model that arrived after the structure did.

132
00:10:46,150 --> 00:10:50,853
And is ready is what stops half-bound components from flickering while that happens.

133
00:10:52,682 --> 00:10:56,180
And this is the same round trip with every piece identified.

134
00:10:56,180 --> 00:11:00,319
The checkbox calls bind updater, which writes to the client data model.

135
00:11:00,319 --> 00:11:02,418
Tapping review calls dispatch action

136
00:11:02,418 --> 00:11:05,682
which produces a client event message on outbound events

137
00:11:05,682 --> 00:11:08,830
carrying the context and the data model's payload map.

138
00:11:08,830 --> 00:11:13,668
Our code posts it, the agent replies, and process message feeds the answer back in.

139
00:11:13,668 --> 00:11:15,650
Every step is a name you now know.

140
00:11:15,650 --> 00:11:19,699
One layer we have not opened, and for some of you it is the most important one.

141
00:11:19,699 --> 00:11:25,771
The engine artifact is thirty types and you rarely construct any of them directly, but two are worth knowing.

142
00:11:25,771 --> 00:11:30,840
The action handler is what the processor builds internally: it holds your interceptor chain

143
00:11:30,840 --> 00:11:35,464
a way to read the current data model, and a way to send a client to server message.

144
00:11:35,464 --> 00:11:37,358
Every user action goes through it.

145
00:11:37,358 --> 00:11:43,040
And an action interceptor is a single suspending function that receives a user action and returns one.

146
00:11:43,040 --> 00:11:45,658
It can pass it through, rewrite it, or hold it.

147
00:11:45,658 --> 00:11:50,671
If you work somewhere that has to prove what left the device, this is your seam: redaction

148
00:11:50,671 --> 00:11:54,403
audit logging, or a human approval step all live here, in one place

149
00:11:54,403 --> 00:11:56,632
rather than scattered across components.

150
00:11:56,632 --> 00:11:58,470
The error path is the other half.

151
00:11:58,470 --> 00:12:02,871
The runtime throws A2UI exception, a component can report one through its scope

152
00:12:02,871 --> 00:12:10,224
and process error sends a client error message back so the agent learns that its payload failed rather than watching a blank screen.

153
00:12:18,299 --> 00:12:20,656
Everything so far came out of the artifacts.

154
00:12:20,656 --> 00:12:23,602
Here is the same API the way you will actually meet it.

155
00:12:23,602 --> 00:12:27,377
First, a practical warning that will cost you an afternoon otherwise.

156
00:12:27,377 --> 00:12:30,441
This project needs Android Gradle Plugin nine point four

157
00:12:30,441 --> 00:12:34,326
and the Gradle command line will happily build it on any Studio version

158
00:12:34,326 --> 00:12:37,007
because Gradle does not care what I D E you have.

159
00:12:37,007 --> 00:12:38,375
Android Studio does care.

160
00:12:38,375 --> 00:12:43,190
On a twenty twenty five release the sync fails outright with an incompatible A G P error

161
00:12:43,190 --> 00:12:46,200
so you get no indexing, no completion and no navigation

162
00:12:46,200 --> 00:12:49,920
while the command line keeps working and tells you nothing is wrong.

163
00:12:49,920 --> 00:12:51,945
You want a twenty twenty six release.

164
00:12:51,945 --> 00:12:55,283
And add a Gradle wrapper pinned to the version you build with

165
00:12:55,283 --> 00:13:01,301
because without one Studio picks its own and fails with a class not found error that tells you nothing useful.

166
00:13:01,301 --> 00:13:03,300
Once it syncs, this is what you get.

167
00:13:03,300 --> 00:13:09,574
Every A2UI symbol resolves, parameter name hints show you what each argument is, and the inlay hints mark usages.

168
00:13:09,574 --> 00:13:16,680
The two launched effects are the whole client: one streams a surface in, the other collects outbound events and posts them back.

169
00:13:16,680 --> 00:13:22,010
Being able to see the types inline is most of why getting the sync working is worth the trouble.

170
00:13:22,010 --> 00:13:26,439
And here is the fastest way to learn this API. Type processor, then a dot.

171
00:13:26,439 --> 00:13:31,286
The completion list is the entire processor interface with its types spelled out.

172
00:13:31,286 --> 00:13:34,158
Active surfaces, a state flow of surface models.

173
00:13:34,158 --> 00:13:37,329
Outbound events, a flow of client to server messages.

174
00:13:37,329 --> 00:13:42,356
Process message, taking a server to client message. Collect messages. Process error.

175
00:13:42,356 --> 00:13:45,886
Five members, and the signatures tell you how they connect.

176
00:13:45,886 --> 00:13:52,948
With no reference documentation published, completion in a synced project is the most reliable API reference you have.

177
00:13:52,948 --> 00:13:54,447
Two honest gaps to close on.

178
00:13:54,447 --> 00:13:57,393
There is no published API reference for these libraries

179
00:13:57,393 --> 00:14:02,159
which is why this video exists: everything here was read out of the artifacts themselves.

180
00:14:02,159 --> 00:14:08,050
And the alpha has no compatibility promise, so pin all five to the same version and expect signatures to move.

181
00:14:08,050 --> 00:14:14,906
The pieces most likely to shift are the ones with the least documentation, which is the property system and the component scope.

182
00:14:14,906 --> 00:14:16,566
If you are building on this now

183
00:14:16,566 --> 00:14:23,046
wrap your own thin layer over the processor and the scope rather than sprinkling library calls through your feature code.

184
00:14:23,046 --> 00:14:25,403
Then when alpha two lands, you fix one file.

185
00:14:25,403 --> 00:14:28,616
If you are starting on Monday, here is the order that works.

186
00:14:28,616 --> 00:14:33,222
Get a project syncing in a twenty twenty six Studio with a pinned Gradle wrapper first

187
00:14:33,222 --> 00:14:36,489
because without indexing you are guessing at every signature.

188
00:14:36,489 --> 00:14:41,738
Render a static payload from assets next, so you learn the message types with nothing else moving.

189
00:14:41,738 --> 00:14:44,844
Only then point it at a real agent and add outbound events

190
00:14:44,844 --> 00:14:49,450
because debugging streaming and the round trip at the same time is how you lose a day.

191
00:14:49,450 --> 00:14:53,252
Keep your own surfaces pre-authored on the server rather than generated

192
00:14:53,252 --> 00:14:59,036
which costs you nothing and means you can show someone exactly what a screen will look like before it ships.

193
00:14:59,036 --> 00:15:04,285
And when a component renders as a red error chip, check the enum value before you check your code.
